clockwork.toml and the project's skills are wired to bd. Once the format
and script are stable, map the concepts across, migrate any existing bd
data, and update the skill prose that names bd.

Not worth planning in detail before the script exists.


Investigated 2026-09-18. The ticket says "the project's skills", and they are
not the project's — which changes what this ticket is.

Where the wiring actually is:

  ~/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md   10 files naming bd
  ~/.claude/commands/*.md        6 files naming bd
  clockwork.toml                 bd_prefix, and never_touch **/.beads/**

The 16 files under ~/.claude are the global clockwork install, shared by
every project on this machine. They hard-code bd commands rather than
parameterising them — bd ready, bd close, bd dep add, bd dolt push — so there
is no per-project setting that would make them mean pidly here.

And three other projects are actively on beads, all with a live .beads:

  bowmaster        bd_prefix bo
  clockwork-city   bd_prefix cc
  idol             bd_prefix idol

pidly is the only one of the four without .beads. So rewriting the global
prose to say pidly does not migrate this project — it switches the other
three onto a tracker they have not imported, which is a way to lose their
work rather than to move it.

That makes this a decision rather than an edit, and it is the owner's:

- Rewrite the global skills to pidly. Simplest, and breaks three projects
  until each is migrated. 46 now exists, so migrating them is possible, but
  it is three imports and three lots of verification, not a side effect of
  this ticket.
- Project-scoped copies under pidly/.claude/skills, overriding the global
  ones here only. Safe and immediate, and it is sixteen copies of prose that
  will drift from the originals — the copies-go-stale rule this project
  refuses everywhere else, applied to the one place it would hurt most.
- Make the global skills tracker-agnostic: they talk about the tracker, and
  clockwork.toml names which one and what its commands are. Nothing breaks,
  every project keeps what it has, and pidly stops being a special case. The
  largest edit, and it is a change to clockwork rather than to pidly — which
  may mean it belongs in clockwork-city's tracker, not this one.

The third is the right shape. It is also the one this ticket cannot decide
alone, because it changes a tool this repository does not own.

Not blocked on anything in pidly. 46 landed, so the migration half of this
ticket is available whenever the direction is settled.


Scoped 2026-09-18, after the owner chose to make the skills tracker-agnostic.
That is the right shape and it is much larger than the prose. Recording what
it actually costs, because the estimate this ticket was picked up on was
wrong by an order of magnitude.

The prose is the small half:

  27 files under clockwork-city   118 mentions of bd
  skills, commands and the Apostle and Factotum definitions
  plus SPEC.md 11.2, which is where the clockwork.toml schema is written down

The source is clockwork-city, not ~/.claude. install.sh copies skills and
commands into ~/.clockwork and from there into ~/.claude, so the installed
copies are output. They are also behind the source today — plan.md and
planning-and-task-breakdown have a membership-versus-ordering distinction in
the repository that the installed copies do not — so an edit made in
~/.claude would be both lost and a clobber of newer work.

The half that is not prose, and the reason this cannot be done here:

  internal/bd      a concrete Client struct, no interface
  cmd/cog/bd.go    613 lines
  ~1500 lines total, ~937 references across the Go sources
  used by convoy, supervisor, lookout, edict, mail, anchor, session, spec

cog does not merely mention bd; it is a beads client. convoy enumerates a
spec's children with bd.DepList and bd.Dependents and claims issues with
bd.Update. So a pidly project cannot run a convoy however the prose is
worded, and changing only the prose would produce documents that describe a
tracker the tool cannot read — which is worse than the honest coupling that
is there now.

Doing it properly is: extract a Tracker interface, implement it for beads and
for pidly, thread it through ten call sites, then the 27 files and the schema.
That is a feature in clockwork-city, wants a ticket in clockwork-city's own
tracker, and is not a pidly change at all.

What belongs to this ticket, once clockwork can name a tracker:

  clockwork.toml   [project.tracker] kind = "pidly", absent meaning beads so
                   bowmaster, clockwork-city and idol keep what they have and
                   need no edit
  clockwork.toml   bd_prefix and never_touch **/.beads/**, which pidly has
                   and does not use

Nothing was changed in clockwork-city. Blocked there, not here.
